# Express.js REST API

## Async/await

- Use `async/await` only — never `.then()` / `.catch()` chains
- Wrap async route handlers so errors reach Express error middleware

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD
router.get('/users/:id', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const user = await userService.getById(req.params.id);
    res.status(200).json({ success: true, data: user });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});
```

## Validation

- Validate `req.body`, `req.params`, and `req.query` in middleware **before** the controller runs
- Return `400` with a clear message when validation fails; do not run business logic on invalid input

## Controllers and services

- **Controllers**: parse request, call service, send response — no DB queries or business rules
- **Services**: business logic, data access, and reusable operations
- Keep controllers thin; move logic to `services/`

## JSON responses

Use a consistent shape:

```javascript
// Success
{ "success": true, "data": { ... } }

// Error
{ "success": false, "error": { "message": "...", "code": "VALIDATION_ERROR" } }
```

## HTTP status codes

| Code | Use |
|------|-----|
| `200` | GET success, PUT/PATCH update success |
| `201` | POST created |
| `204` | DELETE success (no body) |
| `400` | Validation / bad request |
| `401` | Not authenticated |
| `403` | Forbidden |
| `404` | Resource not found |
| `409` | Conflict (duplicate, state conflict) |
| `500` | Unexpected server error (never leak stack traces to clients) |

## Error handling

- Pass errors to Express via `next(err)` — do not send `500` inline in every route
- Use centralized error middleware to map known errors to status codes and JSON responses
- Log full error details server-side; return safe messages to clients
